
// It could be useful for functions to be able to return different types, or multi-return


// Functions currently are like this:
foo :: int(){
    return 4;
}

// But we could allow you to define one function that returns more than one type:

// The `any` type might work, but would require storing an attached type with
// every value, and quickly leads to complexity
foo :: any(x : bool){
    if(x)
        return "Hello!";
    else
        return 2;
}

// Also requires that the type it is placed in is also of type `any`:
res : any = foo(true);



// Another way would be by explicitly giving a set of types the return value can be.
// This reduces the sources of possible errors significantly, but does still have some issues
foo :: [int, string](x : bool){
    if(x)
        return "Hello!";
    else
        return 2;
}

// Then what is this? And how would you access the values of it?
res : int, string = foo(true);



// I think for now, it could be best to use multiple-return values. This basically introduces
// no issues but the syntax will need work.

// You could define it using the syntax above:
foo :: [int, string](x : bool){
    if(x)
        return 0, "Hello!";
    else
        return 2, "";
}

// Then you need to group the definitions
resA : int, resB : string = foo(true);



// But, depending on how arrays are introduced, the above function definition might not work.
// If array types are added as a builtin language feature, it could make [int, string] too ambiguous

// For example, assuming you can define an array by using the [] symbol like:
someArr : int[];

// Then a function that returns an array would be:
bar :: int[](){
    return {};
}

// So maybe multi-return should be defined like:
foo :: int, string(x : bool){
    // ...
}
// Which removes the brackets. The main issue with this is that it then makes lambdas pretty messy.
// For example, if you call a multi-return lambda and want to use its return as an argument, it breaks:
someFunc(int, string(x : bool){ return 4, ""; }(true));
// So multi-return could also be defined like:
foo :: (int, string)(x : bool){
    // ...
}
// Then the lambda case would be:
someFunc((int, string)(x : bool){ return 4, ""; }(true));
// Which uses a lot of paren groups honestly.
// So maybe I'll stick to []:
foo :: [int, string](x : bool){
    // ...
}
// Then the lambda case would be:
someFunc([int, string](x : bool){ return 4, ""; }(true));

// Which gets more complicated if arrays are in the types, but shouldnt break:
foo :: [int[], string[]](x : bool){
    // ...
}


